  "response": "## Yes — the repo ships no agent context\n\n`crime_00007` — **Missing Agent Context**, `medium` / confidence 0.90, agent_risk 0.47. It's the only `medium`-or-higher finding in the top ten by aggregate risk; everything ranked above it is `low`.\n\nEvidence from the scan, which I confirmed against the fixture:\n\n- no `AGENTS.md`, no `CLAUDE.md`, no `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`, no `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- `package.json` declares two bins (`tsup`, `tsup-node`) — this is a tool other people run, and the detector only fires on repos with a published bin for exactly that reason\n\nTwo things worth knowing before you read too much into the output:\n\n- The finding is filed against `assets/cjs_shims.js`. That file is irrelevant — it's just the lexicographically first indexed file, used as a stable anchor for a repo-level finding in a per-file detector loop.\n- `README.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` **do** exist. The detector doesn't count them, and it's right not to: `CONTRIBUTING.md` is four lines of fork-and-PR etiquette plus a release runbook. It tells you `pnpm test` and nothing about the codebase.\n\n## What this costs the next agent\n\nNothing on this repo is discoverable from a cold start, and three of the traps are visible in the same scan:\n\n**The test suite is not a safety net, but it looks like one.** 12 tests contain zero `expect`/`assert` calls (`crime_00032`–`00043`) and 14 more use only weak matchers. An agent changes `src/utils.ts`, runs `pnpm test`, sees green, and ships. Green here means \"the build didn't crash,\" not \"behaviour is protected.\" No agent will infer that; it has to be written down.\n\n**`pnpm test` runs a full `--dts` build first**, and `vitest-global.ts` shells out to `pnpm i` inside `test/` on every run (hence `testTimeout: 50000`). An agent that doesn't know about `build-fast` + `test-only` will either burn minutes per iteration or conclude the suite is broken when the first run is slow.\n\n**The load-bearing files aren't obvious from the tree.** `src/utils.ts` has fan-in 18 and is 424 lines (`crime_00088`, `crime_00083`); `src/plugin.ts` fan-in 10; `src/log.ts` fan-in 9; `src/index.ts` fan-out 17 with a 293-line `build()`. An agent asked to \"clean up utils\" restructures the single most depended-on module in the repo.\n\n**Formatting is Prettier**, not the bundler this repo happens to be. An agent that assumes the project dogfoods its own tooling for formatting will fight the diff.\n\n## Minimal AGENTS.md that clears the finding\n\nThe check is presence-only — `hasAgentContext()` tests for the path, not the contents, so an empty `AGENTS.md` at root technically clears `crime_00007`. That means the file's usefulness is entirely on you. This is the smallest version I'd actually consider worth writing; every claim is drawn from `package.json`, `vitest.config.mts`, or the scan.\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\n`tsup` — a zero-config TypeScript library bundler powered by esbuild.\nPublished to npm; ships two bins, `tsup` and `tsup-node`.\n\n## Commands\n\npnpm (10.x) is required — the repo pins `packageManager`.\n\n- `pnpm build-fast` — build without `.d.ts`. Use this while iterating.\n- `pnpm test-only` — run vitest against the existing `dist/`.\n- `pnpm test` — full `--dts` build, then vitest. Slow; CI-shaped.\n- `pnpm format` — Prettier. This repo does **not** use ESLint or Biome.\n\nFirst test run installs dependencies inside `test/` via `vitest-global.ts`\nand can take a minute. `testTimeout` is 50s for that reason. Tests must be\nrun against a build — `test-only` alone on a stale `dist/` tests old code.\n\n## Before you edit\n\nHighest-connectivity modules. Prefer additive changes; read the importers\nbefore restructuring:\n\n- `src/utils.ts` — 18 direct importers, 424 lines\n- `src/plugin.ts` — 10 importers (mostly type-only)\n- `src/log.ts` — 9 importers\n- `src/index.ts` — 17 imports out; `build()` is ~293 lines\n\nThe largest functions are where edits go wrong quietly: `build`\n(`src/index.ts`), `runEsbuild` (`src/esbuild/index.ts`, ~237 lines),\n`mainTasks` (`src/index.ts`), `main` (`src/cli-main.ts`).\n\n## Testing conventions\n\nTests live in `test/*.test.ts` and drive the built CLI end to end through\nthe `run()` helper in `test/utils.ts`.\n\n**A green suite is weaker evidence than it looks.** Roughly a dozen tests\nassert nothing at all, and others assert only that output is non-empty.\nIf you change behaviour, add a test that would fail without your change —\ndo not rely on the existing suite to catch a regression.\n\n## Don't\n\n- Don't run `pnpm release` or publish. Releases go dev → main; a GitHub\n  Action publishes. See `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n- Don't add a formatter or linter alongside Prettier.\n- Don't reformat files you aren't otherwise changing.\n```\n\nThat's ~45 lines and covers the four things this repo's own scan says an agent will get wrong. I haven't written it — say the word and I'll add it at the fixture root and re-scan to confirm `crime_00007` clears, though note that would modify a scanner-input fixture, so it may not be what you want here.",
